WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.360
This is a great topic that came up yesterday
during a livestream and it touches on politics,

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.960
it touches on finance, it touches on economics.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.380
So let's let's dove right into it.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.770
Why did the stock market go way up last Thursday
when the news was breaking, 6.6 million more

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:22.920
people have lost their jobs and have filed
for unemployment benefits because one would

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.930
imagine that bad economic news would cause
the stock market to go down, not up precipitously.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:36.140
But the opposite happened, indeed, last Thursday
when the terrible unemployment news broke.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.660
Stocks went absolutely skyrocketing for the
day.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.430
And it has confused a lot of different people.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:47.690
And it's a great opportunity to talk through
many of the different elements that are factors

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:48.690
here.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.079
So there's a few different things going on.

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:56.760
First of all, if you want, you can just write
all of this off as mere speculation by market

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.760
insiders.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.760
Right.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.560
You can argue the stock market doesn't even
really have any connection to the news of

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.780
what's going on within the economy.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.999
It's just traders speculating and pushing
stocks up and pushing stocks down, sometimes

00:01:09.999 --> 00:01:14.209
taking long positions, sometimes taking short
positions when it benefits them.

00:01:14.209 --> 00:01:20.130
And there's just no meaning whatsoever in
market moves related to what's happening in

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.130
the economy.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.279
Now, of course, there's an element of truth
to that.

00:01:24.279 --> 00:01:29.659
There's no doubt that hedge funds, derivative
instruments, a lot of these nonproductive

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:37.039
financial mechanisms can benefit from and
even move markets or exacerbate market moves

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:43.259
without being directly tied to some factual
change on Main Street for the average person.

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:44.371
But it's only a piece of it.

00:01:44.371 --> 00:01:48.399
It's not really a comprehensive analysis and
it lacks some understanding of some pretty

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:49.819
basic economic concepts.

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.789
Now, before I jump into this in more detail,
there's two things that I should mention.

00:01:54.789 --> 00:02:01.149
Number one, any effort to explain why stocks
went up or down on a particular day is a fool's

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:07.539
errand in the sense that there is no real
way to definitively say, here's why the market

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:08.539
was up or here.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:11.290
Here's why the market was down on any particular
day.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.989
We're doing this more as a thought experiment.

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:19.319
Number two, I understand that lots of the
ideas that I'm going to use today are partially

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:26.360
debunked in the sense that standard economic
theory, a rational actor, efficient market

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:32.150
economics has been partially debunked and
that is undeniable.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:37.050
But it still is an OK fret framework as an
entry point to start thinking about some of

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.050
these things.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.040
So all of those disclaimers out of the way,
let's now play the game.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:48.069
So the first thing to understand is that the
stock market is supposed to already reflect

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:51.769
future news that we can expect.

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:57.359
This is a classic economics term called efficient
markets hypothesis.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:04.530
And put very simply, what what efficient markets
hypothesis asserts is that asset prices, the

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:11.530
price of the Dow, the NASDAQ, the S&amp;P 500,
it reflects all available information.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:16.269
Now, sometimes information is available, but
it's about a future event that hasn't happened

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:17.400
yet.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.150
Efficient markets hypothesis argues information
about future events is also priced into the

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.150
stock market.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.249
Now, of course, efficient market hypothesis
is one of those things they teach us early

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:33.889
in economics, academia and to some degree
it is complete and total B.S.

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:39.550
It's only sort of true if the market's really
priced in all future information.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:44.060
There wouldn't be any way to make money in
the market and you know, eventually brought

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.819
to to absurdity.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:51.120
Eventually the sun will expand and engulf
the earth and everybody on the planet will

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.779
die unless we move to some other planet.

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:58.220
If that was priced in, stock markets would
be at zero because we already know that that's

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.340
eventually going to happen.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.389
So, again, it's just an idea.

00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:02.769
It's a framework.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:05.889
It doesn't work 100 percent.

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:10.900
But as applied to the unemployment numbers
from last week, even though the 6.6 million

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.870
additional people filing for unemployment
was announced on Thursday, it was expected

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.889
for more than a week ahead of time.

00:04:18.889 --> 00:04:22.410
And there were predictions about the number
of millions of people that would be filing

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.500
for unemployment.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.550
So arguably, it was already priced into the
market now.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:32.699
That could explain why stocks didn't crash
on the announcement of unemployment numbers

00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:33.880
last Thursday.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.139
But it doesn't really explain why stocks actually
went up drastically on that news.

00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:39.680
That doesn't seem to make sense.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.729
Well, that gets us to layer 2 of the analysis,
which is markets don't like uncertainty, even

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:52.080
though there was the expectation of millions
of people losing their jobs last week.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.220
Once it was announced, the uncertainty was
removed.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.250
So if you expect 6.6 million people to lose
their jobs.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.090
And then it's confirmed that 6.6 million people
lose their jobs.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:08.729
You remove the uncertainty, 6.6 million plus
the uncertainty of will it be more or will

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:15.470
it be less is worse arguably than just knowing
it's 6.6 million people who lost their jobs.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.000
So that could make stocks go up.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.680
Lastly, when you file for unemployment benefits,
you start to get money.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:29.789
So from a market perspective, what is going
to happen to the companies on the stock market

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:34.540
when you learn almost 7 million people are
going to start getting money.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:39.250
They're going to start getting unemployment
benefits coming in, which they will spend

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.240
with a lot of these companies that trade on
the stock market.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.030
That could cause stocks to go up on the news
of 6.6 million new unemployment claims.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:54.180
And then lastly, when you learn that nearly
7 million more people lost their jobs, meaning

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.419
around 17 million people have lost jobs in
just three weeks.

00:05:58.419 --> 00:06:03.569
That's a signal that this is bad enough that
the government might do more stimulus.

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:09.590
And again, stimulus injects money, much of
which goes to the companies that are traded

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:11.470
on various stock exchanges.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:18.170
So thinking about it this way, we at least
have a plausible explanation for why terrible

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:22.789
unemployment news correlates with the stock
market going up.

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:29.030
Lastly, remember, this is more proof that
the stock market going up doesn't mean that

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:33.060
Main Street or the average worker individually
is doing well.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.550
Six trillion in stimulus could be great in
the aggregate for the market.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:42.870
But if the individual person is getting a
twelve hundred dollar check seven weeks after

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.540
they've been sitting at home or whatever,
the case may be a bit maybe more like five

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.990
weeks in some cases.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:52.850
You can very quickly see how aggregate market
stimulus can be very good for the stock market,

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:57.009
but not that good for the individual person
who has way more than twelve hundred dollars

00:06:57.009 --> 00:07:02.250
in bills overdue and is just now getting their
twelve hundred dollar check.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.889
Let me know if this type of segment is useful
or interesting.

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:09.099
And you know, I try to explain.

00:07:09.099 --> 00:07:11.270
This is the economic concept.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.430
It's partially B.S., right?

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:18.050
I mean, a lot of standard economics is partially
B.S., but it can still serve to be useful

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.900
in thinking about a particular news event
in this case, bad unemployment news combined

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:22.919
with the stock market going up.

